export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"apparel","name":"Apparel","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"apparel-start","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Apparel: getting started","summary":"What the Apparel add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how a pattern is kept in an ordinary DWG or DXF file.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["apparel","add-on","pattern making","sewing pattern","garment","clothing","piece","grading","marker","dxf-aama"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-piece","text":"Your first piece","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EApparel is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It is for pattern making: you draw the sew line of each pattern piece with CadCraft&#039;s own tools, and Apparel turns it into a piece with notches, a grain line, drill holes, buttons and buttonholes, a seam allowance and a label.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-piece\"\u003EYour first piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the sew line as a closed polyline (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERECTANG\u003C/code\u003E). Lines and arcs that join end to end work too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECE\u003C/code\u003E, pick the outline, give the seam allowance (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E for a net pattern) and a name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENOTCH\u003C/code\u003E and click on the edges where notches go. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRAIN\u003C/code\u003E to draw the grain line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:apparel/apparel-pieces\" data-topic=\"addon:apparel/apparel-pieces\"\u003EPattern pieces\u003C/a\u003E for every mark a piece can carry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA piece is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name \u003Ccode\u003ECC_APPAREL\u003C/code\u003E. So a pattern saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as the drawn piece, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. With Apparel switched on, the pieces are pieces again.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Apparel is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It is for pattern making: you draw the sew line of each pattern piece with CadCraft's own tools, and Apparel turns it into a piece with notches, a grain line, drill holes, buttons and buttonholes, a seam allowance and a label. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Apparel. Its tools are in the Apparel section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › Apparel, as a group at the end of the toolbar. Your first piece Draw the sew line as a closed polyline (PLINE with Close, or RECTANG). Lines and arcs that join end to end work too. Type PIECE, pick the outline, give the seam allowance (or None for a net pattern) and a name. Type NOTCH and click on the edges where notches go. Type GRAIN to draw the grain line. See Pattern pieces for every mark a piece can carry. Files open anywhere A piece is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name CC_APPAREL. So a pattern saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as the drawn piece, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. With Apparel switched on, the pieces are pieces again."},{"slug":"apparel-pieces","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Pattern pieces","summary":"Make a pattern piece from an outline, and add notches, a grain line, a fold line, drill holes, buttons, buttonholes and internal lines.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["piece","notch","grain line","fold","mirror line","drill hole","button","buttonhole","internal line","dart","label","cut quantity"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-piece","text":"Make a piece","level":2},{"id":"marks-on-a-piece","text":"Marks on a piece","level":2},{"id":"edit-a-piece","text":"Edit a piece","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-piece\"\u003EMake a piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the \u003Cstrong\u003Esew line\u003C/strong\u003E as a closed polyline, a circle, or lines and arcs that join end to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECE\u003C/code\u003E and pick it (or pick it first, then type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECE\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the seam allowance, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E for a net pattern, and the piece name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery corner of the outline is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecorner\u003C/strong\u003E; the points along an arc become \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurve points\u003C/strong\u003E, so curves stay smooth when you move them. The piece gets a grain line through its middle, up the page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-on-a-piece\"\u003EMarks on a piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it adds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENOTCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea notch where you click on an edge: slit, V or T (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E), with its depth (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E). Notches sit on the cut line and stay the same distance along their edge when the piece changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRAIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe grain line, from one click to the next; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrowheads\u003C/span\u003E chooses both ends, one end or none\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFOLDLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe fold line on the edge you click: the piece is cut on the fold, and its label says so\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDRILL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edrill holes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBUTTON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebuttons, with their diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBUTTONHOLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebuttonholes, from one end to the other\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINTLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean internal line: darts, pocket and placement lines; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E makes it a smooth curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach of these asks for the piece first (or uses the selected one), then keeps placing until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes away the nearest one. One command is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-piece\"\u003EEdit a piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a \u003Cstrong\u003Egrip\u003C/strong\u003E to move a point of the sew line, an end of the grain or fold line, a drill hole, a button or the label. The cut line and the notches follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern piece\u003C/span\u003E section has the piece name, style, size, cut quantity, fabric, category, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut as a mirrored pair\u003C/span\u003E, the grain line&#039;s arrowheads and the seam allowance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E work on pieces as on any object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E turns a piece into plain lines and text, as in other CAD programs: it is no longer a piece.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Make a piece Draw the sew line as a closed polyline, a circle, or lines and arcs that join end to end. Type PIECE and pick it (or pick it first, then type PIECE). Give the seam allowance, or None for a net pattern, and the piece name. Every corner of the outline is a corner; the points along an arc become curve points, so curves stay smooth when you move them. The piece gets a grain line through its middle, up the page. Marks on a piece Command What it adds NOTCH a notch where you click on an edge: slit, V or T (Kind), with its depth (Depth). Notches sit on the cut line and stay the same distance along their edge when the piece changes. GRAIN the grain line, from one click to the next; Arrowheads chooses both ends, one end or none FOLDLINE the fold line on the edge you click: the piece is cut on the fold, and its label says so DRILL drill holes BUTTON buttons, with their diameter BUTTONHOLE buttonholes, from one end to the other INTLINE an internal line: darts, pocket and placement lines; Curve makes it a smooth curve Each of these asks for the piece first (or uses the selected one), then keeps placing until you press Enter. Remove takes away the nearest one. One command is one undo step. Edit a piece Drag a grip to move a point of the sew line, an end of the grain or fold line, a drill hole, a button or the label. The cut line and the notches follow. In Properties, the Pattern piece section has the piece name, style, size, cut quantity, fabric, category, Cut as a mirrored pair, the grain line's arrowheads and the seam allowance. MOVE, ROTATE, MIRROR and COPY work on pieces as on any object. EXPLODE turns a piece into plain lines and text, as in other CAD programs: it is no longer a piece."},{"slug":"apparel-seam-allowance","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Seam allowance","summary":"Give a piece its seam allowance, a different width on any edge, and choose how the cut lines meet at each corner.","section":"tasks","order":11,"keywords":["seam allowance","cut line","sew line","hem","corner","mitre","square","turned back","net pattern"],"headings":[{"id":"the-allowance","text":"The allowance","level":2},{"id":"corners","text":"Corners","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-allowance\"\u003EThe allowance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA piece keeps its \u003Cstrong\u003Esew line\u003C/strong\u003E; the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut line\u003C/strong\u003E is drawn round it at the seam allowance and is drawn again after every change. A piece with no allowance is a net pattern: its cut line is the sew line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENew pieces get the width in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeam allowance for new pieces\u003C/span\u003E (10 mm, or 1/2&quot; in a drawing in inches), or the one you type at \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange it for the whole piece in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeam allowance\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corners\"\u003ECorners\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the cut lines of two edges meet, the corner can:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERule\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe cut\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFollow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe two cut lines run on until they meet (the usual corner)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe wider allowance is cut off square to its edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMitre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Estraight across, from where one cut line ends to where the next begins\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReverse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturned back: the wider allowance (a hem) is folded back over the sew line, so its end follows the next edge&#039;s cut line as a mirror image; the hem then fits when it is turned up\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInside corners always trim the two cut lines where they cross.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The allowance A piece keeps its sew line; the cut line is drawn round it at the seam allowance and is drawn again after every change. A piece with no allowance is a net pattern: its cut line is the sew line. New pieces get the width in Settings › CadCraft › Apparel › Seam allowance for new pieces (10 mm, or 1/2\" in a drawing in inches), or the one you type at PIECE. Change it for the whole piece in Properties › Seam allowance. Corners Where the cut lines of two edges meet, the corner can: Rule The cut Follow the two cut lines run on until they meet (the usual corner) Square the wider allowance is cut off square to its edge Mitre straight across, from where one cut line ends to where the next begins Reverse turned back: the wider allowance (a hem) is folded back over the sew line, so its end follows the next edge's cut line as a mirror image; the hem then fits when it is turned up Inside corners always trim the two cut lines where they cross."},{"slug":"apparel-grading","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Sizes and grading","summary":"Give a style its size range and base size, put grade rules on points, and see every size stacked in the nest view.","section":"tasks","order":12,"keywords":["grading","grade rule","size range","base size","nest","sizes","rule table","proportional","flip","copy rule","paste rule"],"headings":[{"id":"size-range-and-base-size","text":"Size range and base size","level":2},{"id":"grade-rules","text":"Grade rules","level":2},{"id":"rule-tools","text":"Rule tools","level":2},{"id":"nest-view","text":"Nest view","level":2},{"id":"the-grade-rule-library","text":"The grade rule library","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"size-range-and-base-size\"\u003ESize range and base size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA style has a \u003Cstrong\u003Esize range\u003C/strong\u003E (its sizes in order) and a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebase size\u003C/strong\u003E: the size you draw the pieces in. Every piece with the same style name shares the range.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a piece and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRADE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESizes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the sizes in order, for example \u003Ccode\u003EXS S M L XL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E34 36 38 40 42\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the base size. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the middle one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrading\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel: type the range in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize range\u003C/span\u003E and choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase size\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grade-rules\"\u003EGrade rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Egrade rule\u003C/strong\u003E belongs to a point and says how far it moves, in x and y, in each size, measured from the base size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRADE\u003C/code\u003E, click a point and type its move per size, for example \u003Ccode\u003E10,0\u003C/code\u003E: one size up moves it 10 right, one size down 10 left. Type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E to give each size its own move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrading\u003C/span\u003E panel, choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E and type the moves in the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA corner with no rule stays where it is. A curve point with no rule moves in proportion between the points of its edge that do move, so curves stay smooth when you grade only the corners. Notches keep their distance along their edge in every size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rule-tools\"\u003ERule tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecopy one point&#039;s rule onto other points (other pieces too, matched by size name)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHorizontal flip\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertical flip\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturn a rule round in x or y (the other side of a symmetric piece)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBetween, in proportion\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe points between two points get rules in proportion to their distance along the edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe point no longer moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nest-view\"\u003ENest view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest\u003C/span\u003E (or the panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENest view\u003C/span\u003E switch) draws every size on the base, each in its own colour, so you can check the grading. Change the base size and every size follows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-grade-rule-library\"\u003EThe grade rule library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERules you use again and again (a shoulder, a side seam) can be kept by name. The library is yours: it stays in this browser and works in every drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave:\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrading\u003C/span\u003E panel, type a name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E; or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRADERULE\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E and click the point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EApply:\u003C/strong\u003E choose a saved rule in the panel and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E it to the point shown; or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRADERULE\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E, type its name and click the points. A rule saved for other sizes is matched by size name and measured from the piece&#039;s base size; sizes the rule does not have stay where the base is. Rules saved in millimetres work in a drawing in inches, and the other way round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport / Import:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRADERULE\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E writes the library as JSON (everything) or as a RUL file (the rules that share one size range); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads either, so another program&#039;s rule table becomes rules you can apply.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Size range and base size A style has a size range (its sizes in order) and a base size: the size you draw the pieces in. Every piece with the same style name shares the range. Select a piece and type GRADE, then Sizes. Type the sizes in order, for example XS S M L XL or 34 36 38 40 42. Give the base size. Enter takes the middle one. Or use the Grading section of the right panel: type the range in Size range and choose the Base size. Grade rules A grade rule belongs to a point and says how far it moves, in x and y, in each size, measured from the base size. At GRADE, click a point and type its move per size, for example 10,0: one size up moves it 10 right, one size down 10 left. Type T to give each size its own move. In the Grading panel, choose the Point and type the moves in the table. A corner with no rule stays where it is. A curve point with no rule moves in proportion between the points of its edge that do move, so curves stay smooth when you grade only the corners. Notches keep their distance along their edge in every size. Rule tools Tool What it does Copy / Paste copy one point's rule onto other points (other pieces too, matched by size name) Horizontal flip / Vertical flip turn a rule round in x or y (the other side of a symmetric piece) Between, in proportion the points between two points get rules in proportion to their distance along the edge Remove the point no longer moves Nest view Nest (or the panel's Nest view switch) draws every size on the base, each in its own colour, so you can check the grading. Change the base size and every size follows. The grade rule library Rules you use again and again (a shoulder, a side seam) can be kept by name. The library is yours: it stays in this browser and works in every drawing. Save: in the Grading panel, type a name and choose Save; or GRADERULE › Save and click the point. Apply: choose a saved rule in the panel and Apply it to the point shown; or GRADERULE › Apply, type its name and click the points. A rule saved for other sizes is matched by size name and measured from the piece's base size; sizes the rule does not have stay where the base is. Rules saved in millimetres work in a drawing in inches, and the other way round. Export / Import: GRADERULE › Export writes the library as JSON (everything) or as a RUL file (the rules that share one size range); Import reads either, so another program's rule table becomes rules you can apply."},{"slug":"apparel-measuring","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Measuring and seam walking","summary":"Measure along a sew line, walk one seam against another to check lengths and notches, and list every piece's perimeter and area in every size.","section":"tasks","order":13,"keywords":["measure","seam length","seam walking","walk","notch alignment","perimeter","area","piece table","csv","fabric"],"headings":[{"id":"length-along-a-sew-line","text":"Length along a sew line","level":2},{"id":"walk-a-seam","text":"Walk a seam","level":2},{"id":"piece-table","text":"Piece table","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"length-along-a-sew-line\"\u003ELength along a sew line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESEAMLENGTH\u003C/code\u003E, pick the piece, then click two places on its sew line. CadCraft gives the length both ways round the piece, and the same in every size when the piece is graded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk-a-seam\"\u003EWalk a seam\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeam walking checks that two edges that are sewn together match.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWALK\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first piece on the edge to check, then the second piece on its matching edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft gives both lengths and the difference, and for each pair of notches how far apart they land. The second edge is drawn laid along the first while you look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe two pieces are sewn face to face, so the second edge is walked from its far end. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E walks it the other way; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E checks another size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"piece-table\"\u003EPiece table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECETABLE\u003C/code\u003E lists every pattern piece (or only the selected ones) in every size: the sew line and cut line perimeter and area. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E downloads it for a spreadsheet; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E offers the same file as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel piece table (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E. Areas are in square centimetres, or square inches in a drawing in inches.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Length along a sew line Type SEAMLENGTH, pick the piece, then click two places on its sew line. CadCraft gives the length both ways round the piece, and the same in every size when the piece is graded. Walk a seam Seam walking checks that two edges that are sewn together match. Type WALK. Click the first piece on the edge to check, then the second piece on its matching edge. CadCraft gives both lengths and the difference, and for each pair of notches how far apart they land. The second edge is drawn laid along the first while you look. The two pieces are sewn face to face, so the second edge is walked from its far end. Reverse walks it the other way; Size checks another size. Piece table PIECETABLE lists every pattern piece (or only the selected ones) in every size: the sew line and cut line perimeter and area. CSV downloads it for a spreadsheet; EXPORT offers the same file as Apparel piece table (CSV). Areas are in square centimetres, or square inches in a drawing in inches."},{"slug":"apparel-printing","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Printing patterns","summary":"Print a pattern at full size on a plotter, or tiled on A4 or Letter pages with overlap, match marks and a test square, in the sizes you choose.","section":"tasks","order":14,"keywords":["print","plot","tiled","tiles","home printer","a4","letter","plotter","full size","test square","match marks","pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"print","text":"Print","level":2},{"id":"tiled-pages","text":"Tiled pages","level":2},{"id":"full-size","text":"Full size","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"print\"\u003EPrint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the pieces to print (nothing selected = every piece).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPATTERNPRINT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETiled for a home printer\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull size on a plotter\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the sizes to print (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E = every size).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor tiled pages, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA4\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELetter\u003C/span\u003E and the overlap between pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get one PDF. Pieces print at 1:1, as true lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPrint at \u003Cstrong\u003E100 %\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Eactual size\u003C/strong\u003E. &quot;Fit to page&quot; shrinks the pattern. Measure the test square before you cut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tiled-pages\"\u003ETiled pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach size starts with a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecover page\u003C/strong\u003E: a test square (10 cm, or 4 in in a drawing in inches) and a map of the pages. Then one page per tile that has part of the pattern on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPages are named by \u003Cstrong\u003Erow letter\u003C/strong\u003E (from the top) and \u003Cstrong\u003Ecolumn number\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EA1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EB1\u003C/code\u003E. Each page names its neighbours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrim each page on its outer line. The dashed lines show where the next page overlaps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELay the pages out by their names, overlap them so the \u003Cstrong\u003Ediamonds\u003C/strong\u003E meet (each diamond is labelled with the two pages it joins, \u003Ccode\u003EA1|A2\u003C/code\u003E), and tape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"full-size\"\u003EFull size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach size is one page as big as the pieces, for a plotter or a print shop. A PDF page can be up to 5.08 m (200 in) long; print bigger patterns tiled or a few pieces at a time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E offers both as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel pattern, tiled pages (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApparel pattern, full size (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E, with every size.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Print Select the pieces to print (nothing selected = every piece). Type PATTERNPRINT. Choose Tiled for a home printer or Full size on a plotter. Type the sizes to print (Enter = every size). For tiled pages, choose A4 or Letter and the overlap between pages. You get one PDF. Pieces print at 1:1, as true lines. Print at 100 % or actual size. \"Fit to page\" shrinks the pattern. Measure the test square before you cut. Tiled pages Each size starts with a cover page: a test square (10 cm, or 4 in in a drawing in inches) and a map of the pages. Then one page per tile that has part of the pattern on it. Pages are named by row letter (from the top) and column number: A1, A2, B1. Each page names its neighbours. Trim each page on its outer line. The dashed lines show where the next page overlaps. Lay the pages out by their names, overlap them so the diamonds meet (each diamond is labelled with the two pages it joins, A1|A2), and tape. Full size Each size is one page as big as the pieces, for a plotter or a print shop. A PDF page can be up to 5.08 m (200 in) long; print bigger patterns tiled or a few pieces at a time. EXPORT offers both as Apparel pattern, tiled pages (PDF) and Apparel pattern, full size (PDF), with every size."},{"slug":"apparel-dxf-aama","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"DXF-AAMA and ASTM pattern files","summary":"Save a pattern for other pattern programs, cutters and plotters as DXF-AAMA or ASTM D6673, with its grading, and open patterns other programs saved.","section":"tasks","order":15,"keywords":["dxf-aama","aama","astm","d6673","rul","grade rule table","export","import","pattern file","cutter","nested grading"],"headings":[{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"open","text":"Open","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the pieces to save (nothing selected = every piece).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAAMAOUT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF-AAMA\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EASTM D6673\u003C/span\u003E. Ask the person you send it to which one their program reads.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor graded pieces, choose the grading:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERule table (RUL file)\u003C/span\u003E: the base size in the DXF, the grade rules in a \u003Ccode\u003E.rul\u003C/code\u003E file beside it. Send both files.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENested: every size drawn\u003C/span\u003E: every size of every piece in the one DXF. Larger, but nothing to keep together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach piece is a block with its cut line, sew line, turn and curve points, notches (slit, T and V), grain line, fold line, drill holes and internal lines on the layers the format uses, and its name, size, quantity, category and fabric as text. Buttons and buttonholes have no layer of their own in the format: they are written as internal lines with a note.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E also offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF-AAMA pattern, every size drawn (Apparel)\u003C/span\u003E and the ASTM one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open\"\u003EOpen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAAMAIN\u003C/code\u003E and choose the pattern&#039;s DXF file, and its RUL file too when it has one (choose both at once). The pieces come in as pattern pieces:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe sew line, with the seam allowance read per edge from the distance to the cut line (a piece with only a cut line comes in as a net pattern);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Enotches, grain line, fold line, drill holes, internal lines and the piece&#039;s text;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe size range and grade rules from the RUL file, or from the sizes when every size is drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E reads a DXF-AAMA file too (without a RUL file).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file CadCraft saved opens again exactly as it was: Apparel keeps its own data in the file beside the drawn lines. Other programs ignore that data.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA piece with very many sizes and points can be more than one CadCraft piece holds; it then comes in with its curves thinned a little, or in its sample size only, and CadCraft says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Save Select the pieces to save (nothing selected = every piece). Type AAMAOUT. Choose DXF-AAMA or ASTM D6673. Ask the person you send it to which one their program reads. For graded pieces, choose the grading: Rule table (RUL file): the base size in the DXF, the grade rules in a .rul file beside it. Send both files. Nested: every size drawn: every size of every piece in the one DXF. Larger, but nothing to keep together. Each piece is a block with its cut line, sew line, turn and curve points, notches (slit, T and V), grain line, fold line, drill holes and internal lines on the layers the format uses, and its name, size, quantity, category and fabric as text. Buttons and buttonholes have no layer of their own in the format: they are written as internal lines with a note. EXPORT also offers DXF-AAMA pattern, every size drawn (Apparel) and the ASTM one. Open Type AAMAIN and choose the pattern's DXF file, and its RUL file too when it has one (choose both at once). The pieces come in as pattern pieces: the sew line, with the seam allowance read per edge from the distance to the cut line (a piece with only a cut line comes in as a net pattern); notches, grain line, fold line, drill holes, internal lines and the piece's text; the size range and grade rules from the RUL file, or from the sizes when every size is drawn. IMPORT reads a DXF-AAMA file too (without a RUL file). A file CadCraft saved opens again exactly as it was: Apparel keeps its own data in the file beside the drawn lines. Other programs ignore that data. A piece with very many sizes and points can be more than one CadCraft piece holds; it then comes in with its curves thinned a little, or in its sample size only, and CadCraft says so."},{"slug":"apparel-markers","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Markers","summary":"Lay pieces out on the fabric by hand, with the grain kept, turning and mirroring only where the fabric allows it, and see the marker's length and efficiency.","section":"tasks","order":16,"keywords":["marker","lay plan","fabric width","efficiency","nap","one-way","grain tolerance","cutting","consumption","nesting","automatic","MARKERNEST"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-marker","text":"Make a marker","level":2},{"id":"bring-the-pieces","text":"Bring the pieces","level":2},{"id":"lay-them-out","text":"Lay them out","level":2},{"id":"let-cadcraft-place-them","text":"Let CadCraft place them","level":2},{"id":"save-it","text":"Save it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-marker\"\u003EMake a marker\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMARKER\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the \u003Cstrong\u003Efabric width\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the fabric: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny way (may be turned)\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne way (nap or print)\u003C/span\u003E for velvet, corduroy, a directional print and other fabric where pieces must all point the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the \u003Cstrong\u003Egrain tolerance\u003C/strong\u003E in degrees (0 = the grain exactly along the fabric&#039;s length).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the fabric&#039;s lower-left corner and name the marker.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-the-pieces\"\u003EBring the pieces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the pattern pieces (nothing selected = every piece), type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMARKERADD\u003C/code\u003E and pick the marker. Give the sizes and how many of each, for example \u003Ccode\u003ES:1 M:2 L:1\u003C/code\u003E. Each piece comes as many times as its cut quantity says (a mirrored pair comes as a left and a right). They wait in rows above the fabric, already turned so their grain runs along it. A piece with a very long cut line is thinned on the marker to within 0.02 mm (0.001 in), far finer than any cutter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lay-them-out\"\u003ELay them out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove each piece onto the fabric with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E (or drag it). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E turns one round, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E flips one. CadCraft puts a piece back where it was, and says why, when it would:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eoverlap another piece (touching is fine);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ego off the fabric across a selvedge or the start;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehave its grain line off the fabric&#039;s length by more than the tolerance;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ebe turned round, or mirrored, on one-way fabric.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the fabric the marker shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Elength\u003C/strong\u003E, its \u003Cstrong\u003Eefficiency\u003C/strong\u003E (the pieces&#039; area against the fabric used) and how many of the pieces are on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-cadcraft-place-them\"\u003ELet CadCraft place them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMARKERNEST\u003C/code\u003E and pick the marker. CadCraft lays the marker&#039;s pieces on the fabric for you, keeping every rule above, and tries to keep the marker as short as it can. It tries several orders and turns in a few seconds and keeps the shortest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe rest\u003C/span\u003E keeps the pieces you have already put on the fabric where they are and places the others round them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll\u003C/span\u003E starts over with every piece of the marker.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the \u003Cstrong\u003Espace between pieces\u003C/strong\u003E (0 lets cut lines touch, as they may on a marker).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is one step: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E takes it all back. Move pieces by hand afterwards if you see a better spot. Mixed sizes and several garments on one marker usually nest better than one size of one garment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-it\"\u003ESave it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMARKEROUT\u003C/code\u003E saves the marker as \u003Cstrong\u003EDXF\u003C/strong\u003E (one block per piece on the fabric, and the fabric&#039;s outline) for a cutter, or as a \u003Cstrong\u003EPDF\u003C/strong\u003E at full size for a plotter.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Make a marker Type MARKER. Give the fabric width. Choose the fabric: Any way (may be turned), or One way (nap or print) for velvet, corduroy, a directional print and other fabric where pieces must all point the same way. Give the grain tolerance in degrees (0 = the grain exactly along the fabric's length). Click the fabric's lower-left corner and name the marker. Bring the pieces Select the pattern pieces (nothing selected = every piece), type MARKERADD and pick the marker. Give the sizes and how many of each, for example S:1 M:2 L:1. Each piece comes as many times as its cut quantity says (a mirrored pair comes as a left and a right). They wait in rows above the fabric, already turned so their grain runs along it. A piece with a very long cut line is thinned on the marker to within 0.02 mm (0.001 in), far finer than any cutter. Lay them out Move each piece onto the fabric with MOVE (or drag it). ROTATE turns one round, MIRROR flips one. CadCraft puts a piece back where it was, and says why, when it would: overlap another piece (touching is fine); go off the fabric across a selvedge or the start; have its grain line off the fabric's length by more than the tolerance; be turned round, or mirrored, on one-way fabric. Under the fabric the marker shows its length, its efficiency (the pieces' area against the fabric used) and how many of the pieces are on it. Let CadCraft place them Type MARKERNEST and pick the marker. CadCraft lays the marker's pieces on the fabric for you, keeping every rule above, and tries to keep the marker as short as it can. It tries several orders and turns in a few seconds and keeps the shortest. The rest keeps the pieces you have already put on the fabric where they are and places the others round them. All starts over with every piece of the marker. Give the space between pieces (0 lets cut lines touch, as they may on a marker). It is one step: UNDO takes it all back. Move pieces by hand afterwards if you see a better spot. Mixed sizes and several garments on one marker usually nest better than one size of one garment. Save it MARKEROUT saves the marker as DXF (one block per piece on the fabric, and the fabric's outline) for a cutter, or as a PDF at full size for a plotter."},{"slug":"apparel-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:apparel","title":"Apparel: not built yet","summary":"What the Apparel add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","apparel"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic nesting is an assistant, not an optimiser:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMARKERNEST\u003C/code\u003E has not been compared with a marker made by a pattern maker yet, and it does not split pieces across the fold or match plaids.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlaids and stripes\u003C/strong\u003E in markers (matching pieces to a repeat), and \u003Cstrong\u003Esplitting\u003C/strong\u003E a marker across fabric widths.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdding or removing points\u003C/strong\u003E on a piece&#039;s sew line: draw the outline again and use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIECE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPleats, darts that open and close, and walking a seam round a corner\u003C/strong\u003E onto the next edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENotch depth and width per notch kind\u003C/strong\u003E in DXF-AAMA files: the format does not carry them, so a file read in uses your settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EButtons and buttonholes in DXF-AAMA:\u003C/strong\u003E the format has no layer for them; they are written as internal lines with a note.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening our DXF-AAMA files in another pattern program\u003C/strong\u003E has not been tried yet: check one piece in the receiving program before sending a whole style.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVery large graded pieces:\u003C/strong\u003E a piece with very many points and sizes is more than one CadCraft piece holds. On reading, its curves are thinned slightly, or it comes in its sample size only, and CadCraft says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet Automatic nesting is an assistant, not an optimiser: MARKERNEST has not been compared with a marker made by a pattern maker yet, and it does not split pieces across the fold or match plaids. Plaids and stripes in markers (matching pieces to a repeat), and splitting a marker across fabric widths. Adding or removing points on a piece's sew line: draw the outline again and use PIECE. Pleats, darts that open and close, and walking a seam round a corner onto the next edge. Notch depth and width per notch kind in DXF-AAMA files: the format does not carry them, so a file read in uses your settings. Buttons and buttonholes in DXF-AAMA: the format has no layer for them; they are written as internal lines with a note. Opening our DXF-AAMA files in another pattern program has not been tried yet: check one piece in the receiving program before sending a whole style. Very large graded pieces: a piece with very many points and sizes is more than one CadCraft piece holds. On reading, its curves are thinned slightly, or it comes in its sample size only, and CadCraft says so."}]};
